# Closeout — Closing
#
# Process Groups: A Practice Guide, 4.7 Close Project or Phase.

screen: closeout
title: Closeout
phase: Closing
process: 4.7 Close Project or Phase
order: 95
requires: charter,status
teaches: |
  Why closing is a process rather than an absence of activity, and what has to
  happen for a project to actually end. By the end you should be able to explain
  why most personal projects never close and what that costs.
before: |
  The deliverables have to be finished and accepted. Closing a project that
  is not done is just abandoning it with extra paperwork.
after: |
  The next project. Lessons learned are the only part of a closed project
  that keeps working for you.
intro: |
  Closing is a process, not an absence of activity. Deliverables are formally
  accepted, the records are archived, the lessons are written down while they
  are still true, and the project is released.

  Most personal projects never reach this screen. They fade out, which is why
  nothing is ever learned from them.
source: |
  Process Groups: A Practice Guide (PMI, 2022), process 4.7 Close Project or Phase, the only process in
  the Closing process group. Formal acceptance comes from 5.5 Validate Scope;
  lessons learned are collected throughout and finalized here into the lessons
  learned repository.

field acceptance
  prompt: Has the sponsor formally accepted the deliverables? Write who and when.
  check filled: Without formal acceptance the project is finished but not closed, and it stays on someone's list forever.
  label: Final acceptance
  type: text
  origin: standard
  source: |
    4.7 Close Project or Phase: confirmation that the deliverables were
    formally accepted, per the acceptance criteria in the scope statement and
    the approval requirements in the charter.
  why: |
    It is the sponsor's statement that the project delivered what it was
    authorized to deliver. Without it the project is finished but not closed,
    and it stays on someone's list forever.
  if_blank: |
    There is no moment where the project ends, so it never quite does.
  example: |
    Accepted by the sponsor 2026-10-15. Final review verdict: right.

field outcome
  prompt: Did it work? Answer each success criterion from the charter.
  check column:met: An unanswered criterion is a criterion you are avoiding. An honest 'no' is worth more here than a generous 'yes'.
  check column:traces: Say which charter criterion this row answers. A closeout that answers criteria it invented is not a closeout.
  check covers:charter.success_criteria: A success criterion with no row here is a criterion you are avoiding. An honest no is worth more than a missing line.
  label: Outcome against success criteria
  type: table
  origin: standard
  view: log
  title_column: criterion
  tone_column: met
  source: |
    4.7 Close Project or Phase: measuring the project against the measurable
    objectives and success criteria set out in the charter.
  why: |
    This is the charter's §3 answered, one line at a time. It is the difference
    between "we shipped it" and "it worked", and it is where an honest "no"
    is more valuable than a generous "yes".
  if_blank: |
    Success is declared by whoever is most invested in having succeeded.
  example: |
    Every field has a source tag and a why | Yes | Checked on all 12 screens
  column criterion
    label: Success criterion
    type: text
    width: 5
  column traces
    label: Answers
    type: line
    width: 2
  column met
    label: Met
    type: choice
    options: Yes=ok, Partly=warn, No=danger
    width: 1
  column evidence
    label: Evidence
    type: text
    width: 5

field benefits_realized
  prompt: For each benefit the business case promised: has it shown up yet?
  check column:state: An unanswered benefit is the one the project was actually for. Say yes, no, or too early.
  label: Benefits against the business case
  type: table
  origin: standard
  view: log
  title_column: benefit
  tone_column: state
  source: |
    The benefits management plan, measured at closure. 4.7 confirms the
    deliverables were accepted; the benefits owner confirms, later, whether they
    were worth accepting.
  why: |
    The field above answers the charter. This one answers the business case, and
    the two come apart more often than anybody expects: a project that met every
    success criterion and produced no benefit is the most common expensive
    outcome in project management.

    "Too early to say" is a real answer here and is the honest one for most
    benefits on the day a project closes. What is not acceptable is leaving the
    row out.
  if_blank: |
    The project is judged only on whether it did what it said, and the reason it
    was funded goes unexamined.
  example: |
    B1 | Can explain each artifact unaided | Too early | Retest 2026-11-01 | Nyx
  column benefit
    label: Benefit
    type: text
    width: 5
  column state
    label: Realized
    type: choice
    options: Yes=ok, Partly=warn, No=danger, Too early=faint
    width: 2
  column evidence
    label: Evidence or when to recheck
    type: text
    width: 4
  column owner
    label: Owner from here
    type: line
    width: 2

field lessons
  prompt: What would you do differently, and what worked well enough to repeat?
  check rows>=2: Two lessons is the minimum worth carrying forward. A closed project with no lessons teaches the next one nothing.
  check column:action: A lesson that does not say what to do differently is an observation.
  label: Lessons learned
  type: table
  origin: standard
  view: log
  title_column: lesson
  tone_column: kind
  source: |
    Lessons learned register (4.4 Manage Project Knowledge), finalized at
    closure into the lessons learned repository, an organizational process
    asset for future projects.
  why: |
    A lesson is only useful if it says what to do differently next time. "We
    should have communicated better" is not a lesson; "the reviewer got the
    charter after the build started, so his first feedback was about scope"
    is.

    Write them as they happen, not at the end. Memory at closeout is generous
    to the people writing it.
  if_blank: |
    The same mistake is made on the next project, and it feels novel.
  example: |
    One review gate at the end | What to change | Give the reviewer the charter at kickoff and review after M4
  column lesson
    label: What happened
    type: text
    width: 5
  column kind
    label: Type
    type: choice
    options: What worked=ok, What to change=warn, Surprise=accent
    width: 2
  column action
    label: What to do differently
    type: text
    width: 5
  column area
    label: Area
    type: choice
    options: Scope, Schedule, Quality, Risk, Stakeholders, Process, Technical
    width: 2

field archive
  prompt: Where does the record live now, and who owns the product?
  check filled: Without this the record is wherever the last person left it, and the product has no owner.
  label: Archive and handover
  type: text
  origin: standard
  source: |
    4.7 Close Project or Phase: archiving project records and transferring the
    product to operations or to the next phase.
  why: |
    It says where the record now lives and who owns the thing that was built
    once the project stops existing.
  if_blank: |
    The record is wherever the last person left it, and the product has no
    owner.
  example: |
    Record archived in the repository. Product maintained as a personal tool.
